{
  "configuration_id": "config_031",
  "reason": "Evidence: The best RNA-only model (config_005/006, scvi_n200 multiprofile) achieves ~0.650-0.653 accuracy, while the only multimodal experiment (config_030, scvi_n200 + resnet50, multiprofile, avgpool, concat) scores 0.632 — actually below the RNA-only baseline. This suggests concat fusion may be diluting the strong RNA signal with weak image features (resnet50 alone is only 0.208). Question: Does switching from concat to weighted fusion allow the model to down-weight the weak image branch and recover or exceed the RNA-only performance? Why this experiment: config_031 changes exactly one variable (fusion: concat→weighted) relative to config_030, isolating the fusion mechanism. If weighted fusion recovers to ≥0.650, it confirms the image carries marginal signal that needs proper weighting; if it remains below 0.650, we gain strong evidence that resnet50 image features are unhelpful and should pivot to ViT-L (config_036/037). This is the most budget-efficient next step because it directly resolves whether the current multimodal setup is salvageable before committing experiments to a different image encoder."
}

